<TopicPage slug="subjects/surveying/foundational/surveying-mathematics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Surveying is applied geometry with legal consequences: the positions it establishes become
    property boundaries, construction references and maps, and people make expensive decisions
    on the answers. Its mathematics is therefore dominated by one discipline above all others —
    the <strong>management of error</strong>. Every angle and every distance, no matter how good
    the instrument, is slightly wrong; the surveyor's real craft is measuring <em>how</em> wrong
    the network is, arranging the geometry so errors average out rather than accumulate,
    proving the work with closures, and finally distributing the residuals honestly. Beneath
    that sits classical trigonometry — the raw machinery that turns angles and distances into
    coordinates — and, at the planetary scale, the geodesy that quietly corrects the flat-Earth
    assumptions of local work.
  </p>
  <p>
    An example frames the field. A crew must place a building corner exactly 43.267 m from a
    road monument at a bearing of 132°40′. Everything in the sentence is a surveyor's problem:
    the monument itself carries the accumulated error of the network that established it; the
    bearing is an angle measured against a reference direction that (as geodesy will show)
    depends on which "north" you mean; the distance will be corrected for slope, temperature
    and the map projection's scale factor; and the final coordinates will be quoted to the
    millimetre with a defensible uncertainty. Three bodies of mathematics — trigonometry, error
    theory, geodesy — make that sentence a computation instead of a hope.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="errors: systematic, random, blunders — and propagation" widget="equationStep" config={errorCfg} />

  <h2>Geometry and trigonometry: turning angles into positions</h2>
  <p>
    The surveyor's basic output is <strong>coordinates</strong> — eastings and northings (and
    elevations) for each point — and the engine that produces them is trigonometry. Two
    measurements dominate: <strong>distances</strong> (once by steel tape, now almost always by
    electronic distance measurement, EDM, timing light pulses to a prism to the millimetre) and
    <strong>angles</strong> (horizontal and vertical, measured by total station). From an
    instrument station of known coordinates, one bearing and one distance fix the next point:
    with azimuth 132°40′ (measured clockwise from north) and horizontal distance 43.267 m, the
    offsets are ΔN = 43.267 × cos(132°40′) ≈ −29.32 m and ΔE = 43.267 × sin(132°40′) ≈ +31.81 m —
    the cosines and sines of the bearing <em>are</em> the geometry. Repeating this leg after leg
    is a <strong>traverse</strong>, the backbone method of boundary and construction surveying;
    heights ride along through vertical angles: a target 50 m away observed at +30° elevation
    stands 50 × tan(30°) ≈ 28.87 m above the instrument's line of sight, corrected for the
    instrument's own height and for Earth curvature over distance (which is where geodesy
    starts intruding).
  </p>
  <p>
    The classical repertoire extends the same trigonometry. <strong>Triangulation</strong>
    builds networks from measured angles in triangles (the law of sines fixes every side from
    one baseline); <strong>trilateration</strong> does it from measured distances (law of
    cosines); <strong>intersection and resection</strong> fix a point from angles only —
    resection (standing at an unknown point, sighting three known ones, solving for your own
    position) is the classic field trick. Right-triangle checks never disappear: the 3-4-5
    triangle (or any multiple) squares a corner without a theodolite, and stadia reductions,
    grade percentages (a 5 per cent grade rises 5 m per 100 m — an angle of atan(0.05) ≈ 2.86°)
    and circular curve geometry (deflection angles, chord lengths and arc lengths tied by
    radius: arc = radius × central angle in radians) all reduce to the same trigonometric
    bones. The professional discipline is bookkeeping: bearings converted consistently between
    azimuths and quadrants, coordinates carried forward leg by leg, angles summed against their
    theoretical totals (the interior angles of a closed traverse must total (n − 2) × 180°) —
    arithmetic so routine that its failure is itself a diagnostic of something worse.
  </p>

  <h2>Error and uncertainty: the surveyor's real mathematics</h2>
  <p>
    No measurement is exact, and surveying's contribution is the honest taxonomy. A
    <strong>blunder</strong> is a mistake: mistargeting a prism, transcribing a 4 as a 7,
    booking an angle against the wrong station. Statistics cannot fix blunders; procedure
    prevents them — double-centring observations, closing loops, independent checks, and the
    golden habit of computing <em>in the field</em> so a slip is caught while the crew is still
    standing there. A <strong>systematic error</strong> follows a rule and can be corrected:
    a steel tape calibrated 5 mm long adds 5 mm per measurement (a scale correction); a
    collimation tilt biases every zenith angle identically; EDMs demand atmospheric and
    temperature corrections; the level's line of sight demands equal-length sights. The
    systematic category is where the instrument knowledge lives, and correcting it is a
    physics problem. <strong>Random error</strong> is what remains after both: small,
    equally likely in either direction, following the normal distribution — and it is managed
    statistically, never eliminated.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="traverse misclosure vs leg count, length and precision" widget="sliderLab" config={traverseCfg} />
  <p>
    The most useful single fact in the field is how independent random errors combine: they
    add <strong>in quadrature</strong>. If result R = a + b with independent errors σ<sub>a</sub>
    and σ<sub>b</sub>, then σ<sub>R</sub>² = σ<sub>a</sub>² + σ<sub>b</sub>² — errors of
    opposite sign partially cancel, so the total grows with the <em>square root</em> of the
    number of contributors, not the count. The widget above makes this concrete: a traverse of
    six legs, each angle measured to 10 seconds, closes with an expected angular misclosure of
    10 × √6 ≈ 24.5 seconds — not 60 — because roughly half the leg errors came back negative.
    Lengthen the traverse and it wanders more slowly than intuition fears; shorten the legs
    (at fixed leg count) and the <em>position</em> error at closure worsens, because each
    angle error turns into position error proportional to leg length — the reason long,
    well-conditioned legs beat many short ones. Averaging repeated measurements buys precision
    the same way: n repetitions reduce the random component by √n, which is why instrument
    specifications quote angles "per DIN 18723" standard and why crews turn angles both
    face-left and face-right (which also cancels systematic collimation — procedure and
    statistics cooperating).
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that precision and accuracy are the same, or that more decimal places mean a better survey. Precision is repeatability; accuracy is closeness to truth — a systematically short tape produces beautifully precise, confidently wrong distances. The professional standard is neither decimal decoration nor uncorrected repetition, but a stated uncertainty that is defensible: the surveyor certifies how wrong the answer might be, and the closure evidence is the receipt.</p>

  <h2>Closures and adjustment: the surveyor's receipt</h2>
  <p>
    Because error is inevitable, honest surveys are designed to <strong>check themselves</strong>.
    A traverse closes on its starting point (or a second known point), a level loop returns to
    its start height, and the observed-versus-known difference — the <strong>misclosure</strong> —
    reveals the accumulated error before anything is finalised. The closure is compared against
    the specification (a boundary traverse might be held to a linear misclosure of 1 part in
    10,000 or better; a precise levelling loop to millimetres per kilometre); a failure means
    find the blunder, not adjust around it. Passing, the misclosure is <strong>distributed</strong>:
    classically, angular misclosure is spread equally among the angles (each angle is
    statistically identical), and linear misclosure is distributed among the coordinate
    differences in proportion to leg length — the <em>compass rule</em> — though the
    professional gold standard is <strong>least-squares adjustment</strong>, which weights
    every observation by its estimated uncertainty and finds the unique set of corrections
    that minimises their weighted squares, yielding not just adjusted coordinates but rigorous
    error ellipses around every point. The closure logic scales from a two-hour field exercise
    to the national coordinate frameworks — every rigorous position in the world is the
    adjusted outcome of networks that were designed to catch their own mistakes.
  </p>

  <h2>Geodesy basics: when the Earth stops being flat</h2>
  <p>
    Over short lines, flat-Earth arithmetic is fine; over hundreds of metres and more, the
    planet's shape enters the equations. The Earth is modelled as an <strong>oblate
    ellipsoid</strong> (equatorial radius about 6,378 km, flattened by about 1 part in 298 —
    the parameters of WGS84/GRS80 are the modern standard), and surveying mathematics
    inherits three consequences. First, <strong>meridians converge</strong>: lines of true
    north are not parallel, so bearings measured against true north and bearings measured
    against a map grid's north differ by the convergence angle — which grows east-west from
    the projection's central meridian — and grid azimuths must be corrected to true. Second,
    <strong>map projections trade distortion</strong>: a conformal projection (UTM zones,
    national state-plane systems) preserves angles locally but imposes a small scale factor on
    distances — grid distance and ground distance differ by parts per hundred thousand — so
    professional work applies combined scale-and-height factors when converting measurements
    between the ground, the ellipsoid and the grid. Third, <strong>heights are two different
    quantities</strong>: ellipsoidal heights come free from GNSS (positions relative to the
    mathematical ellipsoid), but engineering and law use orthometric heights relative to mean
    sea level — an equipotential surface called the <strong>geoid</strong>, which undulates
    by tens of metres against the ellipsoid. The conversion H = h − N (orthometric =
    ellipsoidal minus geoid separation) is a daily operation, which is why geoid models are
    maintained and updated as carefully as coordinate datums themselves.
  </p>
  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    A short worked passage shows least-squares adjustment earning its reputation over the
    compass rule. Suppose a small loop levels between four benchmarks, with the observed
    height differences carrying unequal weights — a 2 km leg of careful levelling (standard
    deviation, say, 4 mm) and a 0.5 km leg through difficult ground (5 mm). The naive fix
    distributes the loop's 9 mm misclosure in proportion to distance; the least-squares fix
    distributes it in proportion to <em>variance</em>, so the statistically weaker observation
    absorbs more correction — and the same computation yields, as a by-product, the
    covariance matrix of the adjusted heights: an <strong>error ellipse</strong> (in plan
    networks, literally an ellipse) around every point, with axes stating how many
    millimetres the position might plausibly err in any direction. The ellipse is the
    profession's unit of honesty: boundary corners are certified to it, construction
    tolerances are set against it, and network design — adding which observations would
    shrink which ellipses — is optimisation on top of it. Surveying's deepest claim is
    exactly this: not that its numbers are perfect, but that its uncertainty is
    <em>engineered and stated</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    GNSS quietly rewrote the geodesy lesson for practice: a rover receiving satellite signals
    computes on the global ellipsoid directly, and real-time kinematic (RTK) techniques deliver
    centimetre-level positions in seconds — but the positions are only as good as the datum
    they are expressed in (WGS84 as broadcast, national realisations like NAD83 or ETRS89 for
    legal work), and heights still demand the geoid model. The modern surveyor therefore
    computes on a curved reference ellipsoid with the flat-Earth version as a local
    approximation — and the FS exam's mathematics section includes geodesy precisely because
    the approximation now needs to be consciously chosen, not unconsciously assumed. Deeper
    still lie the field's open edges: rigorous least-squares network design (where should
    observations be added to shrink which error ellipses?), atmospheric error modelling for
    GNSS (ionosphere, troposphere, multipath), time-dependent datums as plate tectonics drifts
    continents centimetres per year, and the metrology of certainty itself — because in a
    profession whose outputs become deeds and steel, the mathematics of "how wrong might this
    be?" is not an appendix to the answer. It is the answer.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NCEES FS exam specifications; Ghilani &amp; Wolf, <em>Elementary Surveying</em>; Buckner, <em>Surveying Computations</em>.</p>
</TopicPage>
